WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.969
- Have y'all met Selena before?

00:00:01.969 --> 00:00:03.610
- I have not.
- First time.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:04.930
- Just now.
- Okay, she might've been

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:07.290
the one stalking you Comic
Con or whatever it's called.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.170
CrimeCon.
- CrimeCon.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.370
- There's all these Cons.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.170
I don't know, I need to go to them.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.020
So y'all were called the
Rolling Stones of true crime.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.860
That's a pretty sexy title, y'all.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.129
I'm just sayin'.
- Yeah.

00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:20.137
- We paid the guy.
- You paid for it.

00:00:20.137 --> 00:00:22.330
What does it feel like though?

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.240
If you're Rolling Stones of true crime

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.980
that means everyone watches you.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.450
- It's weird.
- I've been a journalist

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.203
for a long time and that
just doesn't happen.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.660
I certainly didn't see this coming.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.670
When we started doing
true crime stories about,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.280
I don't know, a little bit
more than 10 years ago,

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.640
I didn't wanna do it.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.730
I wasn't interested.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.030
Usually when you're covering cops--

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.430
- Time flies, John.
- It does.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:49.730
- It must've been closer to 20.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.320
- Well, we've been here--
- Who's counting?

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.310
- We've been here 25 years.
- Who's counting?

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.143
You're counting.

00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:56.320
- We've been a dateline 25 years each.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.873
- Wow, you must like it.

00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:01.350
So, you're a big fan, right?

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.183
- Yes.

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:03.460
- Do you have any questions?

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.030
- Okay, what's the craziest
story that you can remember?

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.210
- Oh gosh, there's been so many.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.560
- The one you're on now.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.000
- Yeah sure, you know the
missing kids in Idaho.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.250
The one's the Hawaii connection,
the Arizona, all of it.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.310
So we're chasing around--
- Wait what?

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:19.890
There's missing kids in Idaho and Hawaii?

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:20.723
What did you say?

00:01:20.723 --> 00:01:21.556
I don't know this.

00:01:21.556 --> 00:01:22.834
- You don't know this story?
- No, I don't know this.

00:01:22.834 --> 00:01:24.960
Tell me this story.
- Oh okay, well,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.580
they disappeared in September,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.830
the authorities have been
trying to get the mother

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.660
of those children and her new husband

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.550
to reveal where they are,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:35.950
and if they will just reveal it

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.260
all this might go away.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.150
But, there's also the
question of the new husband's

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.200
ex-wife who mysteriously passed away,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.033
though she was very healthy.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:46.866
- What?

00:01:46.866 --> 00:01:51.100
- The new wife's ex-husband
whose brother shot her.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.090
But then later on her
brother mysteriously died.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.800
- There's this trail of death

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.840
that is following these
people around the country.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.620
- I can't even keep up
with what you just said.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.500
- No, no, no.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.760
- I was literally train A to train B,

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.500
there's so many crazy things

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.607
in what you just said.
- It's the most complicated

00:02:08.607 --> 00:02:09.440
story I've ever--

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.900
- He compressed that into about
two hours, a few weeks ago,

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.520
and it was pretty hard to do then.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.020
- Yeah, it was.
- Oh my gosh.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.950
Do y'all ever have nightmares

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.460
from all the things that you see?

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.600
I always wonder from
investigators or cops,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.770
or journalists, people doing this.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.620
That's why I had to kinda
check out for a bit,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.140
because I started freaking out,

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.089
thinking everyone was gonna kill me.

00:02:29.089 --> 00:02:31.470
I'd lock my doors all the time.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.770
I literally am the friend my assistant

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.280
and my nanny, everybody will tell you.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.390
I make everyone text me when they're home,

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.370
and they've checked their house.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:39.513
This is a real thing.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.640
Y'all don't get affected by it?

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.368
- First of all, that's not a bad idea.

00:02:43.368 --> 00:02:44.709
- Exactly, that's what I said!

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:46.140
- It's not a bad idea, right?
- Josh?

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.240
Yes, it's like they wanna be on your show.

00:02:49.679 --> 00:02:51.698
- That's not a bad idea.
- That's a terrible idea.

00:02:51.698 --> 00:02:53.330
- It is not a terrible idea!
- No, it's an awful idea.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.440
- Buddy system, let
people know you're safe.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.320
- Absolutely.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.750
- But y'all don't get phased by it?

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.760
- No, in fact the crime
rate has gone down.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.450
- Well that's good.
There aren't as many murders

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:04.283
as there used to be.

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:05.116
- Are you bummed?

00:03:05.116 --> 00:03:05.949
Dang it.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.010
Wish there could be more crime.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.540
- Look, you know, you do see

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.500
some terrible things in this job.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.500
You also see some stories
of tremendous strength

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.650
and redemption, and people
who struggle against

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.160
really terrible things
that have happened to them,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.450
and end up doing things

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:30.740
that they wouldn't have thought possible.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.140
So you see families, which are suffering,

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.370
and they manage to come through.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.140
And that's one of the
inspirational things about this,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.660
and the other is this
show's really sort of

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.410
about the best in law enforcement.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:42.430
- It's true
- Yeah.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.480
- I'm telling ya', the
people who solve crimes

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.270
in this country, even though sometimes

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.580
they get a bad reputation once in awhile,

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.760
are overall the most
committed, marvelous people

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.150
you could ever hope to meet.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.073
And especially homicide detectives.

